    "mainEntityOfPage": "https://calcfordiy.com/guides/soil-percolation-test-drywell"
  }
  </script>

  <!-- Breadcrumbs -->
  <nav class="flex text-xs text-gray-500 mb-4 gap-1 items-center not-prose">
    <a href="/" class="hover:text-blue-600">Home</a>
    <span>/</span>
    <a href="/guides" class="hover:text-blue-600">Guides</a>
    <span>/</span>
    <span class="text-gray-800 font-medium">Soil Perk Test</span>
  </nav>

  <!-- Article Header -->
  <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 mb-8 shadow-xs not-prose">
    <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-blue-50 text-blue-700 text-xs font-semibold mb-3 border border-blue-100">
      <span>💧 Geotechnical Science &amp; Hydrology</span>
      <span>•</span>
      <span>ASTM D3385 Infiltration Testing</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-gray-900 tracking-tight mb-3">
      How to Perform a DIY Soil Percolation (Perk) Test for Drywells
    </h1>
    <p class="text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-6">
      Before spending money on plastic drywell basins, gravel, and PVC pipes, you must determine whether your soil can actually absorb stormwater. Sand absorbs gallons per minute; dense clay can take days. A simple percolation test measures your soil's infiltration rate in inches per hour, dictating whether you need one tank or four.
    </p>

    <!-- Quick Stats Grid -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 pt-6 border-t border-gray-100">
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Test Depth</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900 mt-0.5">24" to 36" (Drywell Bottom)</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Good Infiltration Rate</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-emerald-600 mt-0.5">&gt; 1.0 to 3.0+ In/Hour</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Pre-Soak Period</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900 mt-0.5">4 to 12 Hours (Crucial)</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Main Editorial Content -->
  <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6 sm:p-8 mb-8 prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">Step-by-Step DIY Percolation Test Protocol</h2>
    <p>
      Do not perform this test on the surface lawn; you must test the soil layer where the drywell will actually discharge water:
    </p>
    <ol class="space-y-4 not-prose my-6">
      <li class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 text-xs text-gray-700">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">Step 1: Dig the Test Hole to Design Depth</strong>
        Dig a 10-inch to 12-inch diameter hole down to the planned depth of your drywell bottom (typically 24 to 36 inches deep). Scratch the sidewalls and bottom with a trowel to remove any clay smearing caused by the shovel blade. Add 2 inches of clean pea gravel to the bottom to prevent water churning.
      </li>
      <li class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 text-xs text-gray-700">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">Step 2: The Mandatory Pre-Soak Phase</strong>
        Fill the hole with at least 12 inches of clean water and keep it filled for at least 4 hours (or overnight for clay soils). Dry soil acts like a sponge initially, giving a false fast reading. Pre-soaking ensures the soil clay particles swell to realistic saturated winter conditions.
      </li>
      <li class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 text-xs text-gray-700">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">Step 3: Measure Water Drop Over 1 Hour</strong>
        Refill the hole to a depth of 6 inches above the gravel. Lay a 2x4 board across the top of the hole, and place a measuring tape or yardstick into the water against the reference board. Record the water level at time 0, and record the level again after 60 minutes.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">Interpreting Your Soil Perk Results</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 not-prose">
      <table class="w-full text-xs text-left border-collapse border border-gray-200">
        <thead class="bg-gray-100 text-gray-800 font-bold border-b border-gray-200">
          <tr>
            <th class="p-3">Perk Rate (Inches/Hour)</th>
            <th class="p-3">Soil Classification</th>
            <th class="p-3">Drywell Feasibility</th>
            <th class="p-3">Design Recommendation</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 text-gray-700">
          <tr class="bg-emerald-50/60 font-semibold text-emerald-900">
            <td class="p-3 font-bold">&gt; 3.0 in/hr</td>
            <td class="p-3">Coarse Sand / Loamy Sand</td>
            <td class="p-3 font-bold text-emerald-700">Excellent</td>
            <td class="p-3">Standard 1-tank Flo-Well handles 1,000 sq ft roof</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-bold">1.0 to 3.0 in/hr</td>
            <td class="p-3">Sandy Loam / Medium Silt</td>
            <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-blue-700">Good</td>
            <td class="p-3">Increase stone envelope to 18" thickness or use 2 tanks</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-bold">0.5 to 1.0 in/hr</td>
            <td class="p-3">Silt Loam / Moderate Clay</td>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-amber-700">Marginal</td>
            <td class="p-3">Requires 3+ tanks and high-capacity pop-up overflow</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="bg-red-50/60 font-semibold text-red-900">
            <td class="p-3 font-bold">&lt; 0.5 in/hr</td>
            <td class="p-3">Dense Marine Clay / Hardpan</td>
            <td class="p-3 font-bold text-red-700">Unsuitable for Drywell</td>
            <td class="p-3">Drywell will fail; route stormwater to daylight rain garden or ditch</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Jobsite Field Note -->
  <FieldNote
    author="David Miller, PE"
    role="Structural & Civil Engineering Consultant"
    experience="20+ Years Field Experience"
    avatarBg="bg-blue-100 text-blue-800"
    initials="DM"
    title="Why Testing Without Pre-Soaking Ruined Thousands of Drywells"
    note="In dry summer months like August, bone-dry clay soil cracks and contains microscopic vacuum fissures. If you pour a bucket of water into a freshly dug hole without pre-soaking, the water will vanish in 15 minutes! The homeowner mistakenly thinks, 'Wow, I have great drainage!' In reality, as soon as winter rains start, the clay expands, swelling tight like a rubber stopper. The true infiltration rate drops to 0.1 inches per hour, and the drywell overflows on the very first heavy rain. Never skip the 4-hour pre-soak. You must test the soil in its swollen, fully saturated state to get honest, engineering-grade data."
  />

  <!-- Costly Mistakes -->
  <CostlyMistakes
    tradeName="Soil Percolation Testing"
    mistakes={[
      {
        number: 1,
        title: "Testing Only the Topsoil Layer",
        costImpact: "$2,400 (Excavating Useless Drywell Pit in Hardpan)",
        description: "The top 8 inches of garden soil might drain rapidly, but if there is an impermeable blue-clay hardpan layer 24 inches down, water will hit a dead end.",
        solution: "Dig your test hole down to the exact elevation where the bottom of the drywell gravel will rest."
      },
      {
        number: 2,
        title: "Smearing the Hole Sidewalls with a Flat Shovel",
        costImpact: "$650 (Artificially Low False Infiltration Rate)",
        description: "Scraping a metal shovel blade against moist clay polishes the sidewalls, glazing the soil pores shut like pottery.",
        solution: "Use a small hand garden fork or scratch trowel to scarify the bottom and sides of the test hole before pouring water."
      },
      {
        number: 3,
        title: "Ignoring the Seasonal High Water Table",
        costImpact: "$3,500 (Drywell Permanently Submerged in Groundwater)",
        description: "Installing a drywell in an area where the winter water table rises to within 2 feet of the surface means the drywell is already full of water before it rains!",
        solution: "Check local soil survey maps or auger a 4-foot test hole; the bottom of a drywell must stay at least 2 feet above the seasonal high water table."
      }
    ]}
  />

  <!-- Builder Community Reviews -->
  <CommunityReviews
    tradeTitle="Soil Perk Testing Guide"
    reviews={[
      {
        author: "Colton & Amanda R.",
        location: "Annapolis, MD",
        projectScope: "Perk Test for Downspout Infiltration",
        savingsClaim: "Saved $1,200 by Sizing Right",
        feedback: "Our pre-soak test showed 1.8 inches per hour in sandy loam. We used the drywell calculator and found two 50-gallon Flo-Wells with a 12-inch gravel ring would easily handle our 1,400 sq ft roof. No guessing, no flooding.",
        keyTakeaway: "Use a laser level or straight 2x4 with a bubble level across the hole as a fixed reference line for your measuring tape."
      },
      {
        author: "Zach M.",
        location: "Green Bay, WI",
        projectScope: "Catching Heavy Red Clay Soil Early",
        savingsClaim: "Avoided a $4,000 Failed Drywell",
        feedback: "Pre-soaked the hole for 6 hours and after an hour the water had dropped only 1/8 of an inch. Realized our red clay is completely impermeable. Instead of wasting money on a buried drywell that would fail, we routed our downspouts 60 feet to daylight in an engineered rain garden.",
        keyTakeaway: "If your perk rate is under 0.5 inches per hour, don't force a drywell; use daylight piping or surface bioretention swales."
      }
    ]}
  />

